<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/ethics-three-theories">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Philosophy. You need nothing from earlier lessons — no
    philosophy vocabulary, no history of ideas — only the moral judgements you already make
    every day and a willingness to ask what they stand on. Everything else is built here,
    step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state what normative ethics asks — not "what do people approve of?" but "what justifies a moral judgement?" — and why that question survives the reply "it is all just opinion";</li>
    <li>explain the three great families of answer: consequentialism (outcomes), deontology (duty and rules) and virtue ethics (character);</li>
    <li>run Bentham's felicific calculus on a real decision, and say what Mill added and what Kant refused;</li>
    <li>apply Kant's two formulations of the categorical imperative, and explain why they ground human rights more directly than counting does;</li>
    <li>describe Aristotle's eudaimonia, the virtues as habits, and the doctrine of the mean;</li>
    <li>diagnose, in a live moral argument, <em>which</em> consideration — outcome, duty or character — is actually doing the work.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever you make or hear a moral judgement, name what is backing it. Most
    everyday moral arguments are people in different frameworks shouting past each other,
    and the arguing improves the moment someone says which thing they think is decisive.
  </p>

  <h2>A wallet on the pavement</h2>
  <p>
    You find a wallet on the street: cash inside, an identity card with an address. Nobody
    saw you pick it up. What should you do — and, crucially, what would count as a
    <em>reason</em> for doing it? One person says: return it, because a city where wallets
    come back is a city you want to live in, and everyone's wellbeing is better if returns
    are the norm. Another says: return it, because keeping it treats the owner as a means to
    your convenience, and that is wrong whatever the outcome. A third says: the question to
    ask is what an honest person would do — and you would rather become someone who does not
    pause over other people's money.
  </p>
  <p>
    Notice that all three answers are serious, and they are not the same answer. The first
    counts consequences. The second appeals to a duty that holds regardless of consequences.
    The third says nothing about this act at all and everything about the kind of person you
    are becoming. Philosophy's three great answers to "what makes an action right?" are
    exactly these: <strong>consequentialism</strong>, <strong>deontology</strong> and
    <strong>virtue ethics</strong>. They are not three recipes producing the same dinners —
    in hard cases they genuinely disagree — and this lesson takes each in turn.
  </p>

  <h2>What the question is asking</h2>
  <p>
    Normative ethics asks what <em>justifies</em> a moral judgement — an answer that would
    still stand if you had been raised somewhere else. The question sounds abstract until
    you meet its rivals. One rival says rightness is whatever the individual thinks: then
    two people who disagree are both correct, and moral disagreement — the thing you have
    whenever you argue with anyone about anything — becomes impossible in principle. Another
    says rightness is whatever the society approves: then every reformer is wrong by
    definition, and the abolitionist was immoral precisely when they were right. Both rivals
    explain something real (disagreement is widespread; cultures differ) and neither can say
    what the reformer or the dissident is doing <em>right</em>. That failure is why
    philosophers look for principles that can be argued for — reasons that survive being
    questioned.
  </p>
  <p>
    A first sorting of the field: theories divide over <em>where they look</em>.
    <strong>Consequentialist</strong> theories look at what the act brings about — the right
    act creates the most good. <strong>Non-consequentialist</strong> theories look at the act
    itself — some acts are forbidden or required regardless of results, because of the rule
    or duty they fall under. And virtue ethics changes the question: not "which act?" but
    "which character?". The timeline below shows the three answers arriving across
    twenty-five centuries.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the ethics conversation, twenty-five centuries long — filter by era" widget="timeline" config={ethTimeline} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the shape of the conversation. Confucius and the Buddha
    are asking ethical questions centuries before the Greek word exists — ethics was never a
    European possession. Then notice the long gap between Aristotle (c. 350 BCE) and Kant
    (1785): for two thousand years the <em>rules</em> and <em>consequences</em> questions ran
    the field and the character question went quiet, until Anscombe's 1958 paper argued that
    both of the modern answers had lost something Aristotle never forgot. The lesson follows
    the same arc: consequences first, then duties, then the return of character.
  </p>

  <h2>Bentham: pleasure counted</h2>
  <p>
    Jeremy Bentham (1789) built the first systematic consequentialism on a bold
    simplification: the only things ultimately good in themselves are pleasure and the
    absence of pain. Everything else — money, friendship, health, knowledge — is good only
    because it feeds pleasure or blocks pain. This is <strong>hedonism</strong>, and combined
    with the demand to count <em>everyone's</em> pleasure equally (each to count for one,
    nobody for more than one) it becomes <strong>utilitarianism</strong>: the right act is
    the one producing the greatest net happiness for the greatest number. Bentham, true to
    his reforming temperament, made the test mechanical. His <strong>utility (felicific)
    calculus</strong> scores a pleasure along dimensions: intensity, duration, certainty,
    propinquity (how soon), fecundity (will it breed further pleasures?), purity (will it
    cost pains later?) and extent (how many people?).
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked judgement: a city must choose between a park or a car park on one plot. The car
    park pleases 4,000 daily commuters <em>intensely</em> and <em>certainly</em> (propinquity
    high). The park pleases perhaps 40,000 residents weakly, over decades, with high
    fecundity — walks become habits, habits improve health, health compounds. Bentham's
    dimensions do not just rank the options; they expose <em>why</em> we hesitate: the park
    wins on extent, duration and fecundity, the car park on intensity and certainty. His
    calculus was also radical politics. In 1789, arithmetic pleasure-counting of "extent"
    included the pleasure of animals ("the question is not, Can they reason? nor, Can they
    talk? but, Can they suffer?") and argued against slavery and for decriminalising
    homosexuality — verdicts his contemporaries called monstrous and history calls correct.
  </p>

  <h2>Mill: some pleasures are better</h2>
  <p>
    John Stuart Mill, Bentham's godson, inherited the theory and its embarrassments. A theory
    that counts only <em>quantity</em> of pleasure endorses the "doctrine of swine" —
    push-pin (a low parlour game) is as good as poetry if the pleasure is equal — and it
    cannot explain why an intelligent person's misery can outrank a contented fool's
    happiness. Mill's fix (1861/1863) was <strong>qualitative hedonism</strong>: pleasures
    differ in kind, not just amount, and pleasures exercising our higher faculties
    (intellect, imagination, moral feeling) are <em>intrinsically superior</em>. His test is
    democratic, not elitist: ask those who have experienced both kinds, and they reliably
    prefer the higher even at the cost of contentment — hence "it is better to be a human
    being dissatisfied than a pig satisfied; better to be Socrates dissatisfied than a fool
    satisfied."
  </p>
  <p>
    Mill also offered a famous <strong>"proof"</strong> of the greatest happiness principle
    in <em>Utilitarianism</em> (1863): the only proof that something is desirable is that
    people actually desire it; everyone desires their own happiness; therefore happiness is
    desirable — and, he added, each person's happiness is a good to <em>that</em> person, so
    the general happiness is a good to the aggregate of persons. Critics instantly flagged
    the slide: from "everyone desires their own happiness" to "everyone <em>ought</em> to
    promote everyone's happiness" is an inference that needs a premise Mill never quite
    states. Modern utilitarianism largely sidesteps the problem by becoming
    <strong>preference utilitarianism</strong> (associated with Peter Singer): maximise the
    satisfaction of preferences rather than pleasure, which handles the case of the ascetic
    who genuinely prefers less pleasure and more meaning — counting what people want, not
    what makes them smile.
  </p>

  <h2>Act vs rule, and four standing objections</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Act utilitarianism</strong> applies the test case by case: <em>this</em> act,
    <em>these</em> consequences. It is demanding and — critics say — monstrous in the wrong
    hands. <strong>Rule utilitarianism</strong> instead asks: which <em>rules</em>, generally
    followed, maximise utility? Then follow the rules (don't lie, keep promises, don't punish
    the innocent) even when this once, breaking the rule would "pay". It trades a little
    optimisation for stability and trust, though critics press a dilemma: if rules can always
    be overridden when utility demands, it collapses into act utilitarianism; if they can
    never be overridden, why not justify the rules directly?
  </p>
  <p>
    Four classic issues sharpen the choice. The <strong>tyranny of the majority</strong>:
    pure utility-counting can license trampling a minority — the pleasure of a bigoted crowd
    outweighed the pain of the person they scapegoat, which is why Mill himself needed a
    separate work (<em>On Liberty</em>) to carve out rights that utility alone could not
    guarantee. The <strong>experience machine</strong> (Robert Nozick, 1974): imagine a tank
    that feeds you flawless pleasurable experiences — would you plug in for life? Most
    refuse, which suggests we value <em>contact with reality</em>, not just experience of it
    — a problem for any theory that makes experience the sole good. <strong>Calculation</strong>:
    real decisions involve consequences we cannot forecast, preferences we cannot compare,
    and no time to run the calculus. And <strong>partiality</strong>: utilitarianism counts
    every person's utility equally, which condemns spending your Saturday with your own child
    rather than volunteering where help is scarcer — yet partiality to one's own is arguably
    part of what a good human life <em>is</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the consequentialist family looks at what an act produces,
    counts everyone impartially, and has been refined from Bentham's pleasure arithmetic to
    Mill's qualitative ranking to Singer's preferences. It is the closest thing ethics has to
    a public decision procedure — and it has four standing objections it has never quite
    paid off.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> utilitarianism does not mean "the end justifies any
  means" or "maximise pleasure selfishly". The utility counted is everyone's, impartially,
  and rule utilitarians would say the long-run cost of "any means" thinking is catastrophic.
  The theory's real vulnerability is narrower: it cannot obviously rule out <em>any</em>
  sacrifice of an individual if the numbers come out right.</p>

  <h2>Kant: duty and the good will</h2>
  <p>
    Immanuel Kant (1785) starts from the opposite pole. What makes an action good cannot be
    its consequences — those lie outside our control — but the <strong>good will</strong>:
    acting from duty, out of respect for the moral law, rather than from inclination. The
    shopkeeper who is honest because honesty <em>pays</em> acts correctly but not
    <em>morally</em>; the one who gives fair change because it is right, even to a stranger
    who would never know, acts from duty. Kant distinguishes <strong>hypothetical
    imperatives</strong> — "if you want X, do Y", binding only on those who share the goal
    (if you want to be healthy, exercise) — from the <strong>categorical imperative</strong>:
    a command binding unconditionally, because it is what <em>reason itself</em> requires of
    any rational agent.
  </p>
  <p>
    The categorical imperative has two famous formulations. The <strong>universal law</strong>
    formulation: "act only on that maxim which you can will as a universal law." Test a maxim
    — "make a false promise to get money when it suits me" — by imagining everyone doing it.
    The practice self-destructs: if everyone lied, no one would believe promises, and
    promise-making would be impossible. The maxim cannot even be conceived as universal, so
    lying is rationally incoherent, not merely inconvenient. The <strong>humanity</strong>
    formulation: "treat humanity, whether in yourself or others, always as an end, never
    merely as a means." Every promise-keeper, every manipulator, every exploiter uses other
    people's rational agency as a tool; the formula draws the line between cooperation and
    use. It grounds human rights with unusual directness — each person is an end in
    themselves, which is exactly what utility-counting struggles to say.
  </p>
  <p>
    Kantian ethics has famous pains too. <strong>Clashing duties</strong>: Kant held lying is
    always wrong, so when a murderer asks where your friend is hiding, you must tell the
    truth — his opponents (and even sympathetic readers like Benjamin Constant, whose letter
    provoked Kant's chilling reply) see this as the theory failing a basic test of moral
    seriousness. <strong>Foot's challenge</strong>: Philippa Foot (1972) argued Kant was
    wrong that moral demands are categorical — they look hypothetical after all ("if you
    want to be part of a decent human community, keep your promises"), binding on us only
    because we <em>care</em> about what we care about. Whether morality is a demand of reason
    or a contingent shape of caring remains one of the field's live disputes.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that Kant's categorical imperative is just the Golden
  Rule ("do to others what you would have them do to you") in Latin dress. The tests are
  genuinely different. The Golden Rule asks what <em>you</em> would want, which makes the
  standard your own tastes — the masochist loophole is famous. Kant's test asks what rule
  <em>any rational agent</em> could will as universal law, which is why it can condemn things
  you might personally not mind. One is a preference, the other tries to be a constraint on
  preferences.</p>

  <h2>Aristotle: what kind of person?</h2>
  <p>
    Virtue ethics (Aristotle, <em>Nicomachean Ethics</em>, c. 350 BCE) asks the question the
    other two skip: not "which act?" but "which <em>life</em>?" — and answers: the life of
    <strong>eudaimonia</strong>, usually translated "flourishing" or "well-being" but meaning
    something stronger than happiness: living well and doing well, as a human being.
    Aristotle reaches it by the <strong>function argument</strong>: the good of anything that
    has a function lies in performing that function well; what is distinctively human is not
    growth (plants) or perception (animals) but <em>activity of the soul in accordance with
    reason</em>; therefore human good is rational activity, excellently performed, over a
    whole life. Eudaimonia is the <strong>final end</strong> — the one thing we pursue for
    its own sake and not for anything further; money, honour and pleasure are all pursued
    <em>for</em> something else.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Virtues</strong> are stable excellences of character — courage, honesty,
    generosity, justice, temperance — acquired not by lecture but by habituation: do honest
    things until honesty becomes your default, the way a builder becomes skilled by
    building. Each virtue sits between two vices, the <strong>doctrine of the mean</strong>:
    courage between cowardice and recklessness, generosity between stinginess and
    wastefulness, proper pride between servility and vanity. The mean is not bland
    moderation; it is <em>relative to us and to the situation</em> — the courage of a
    firefighter entering a fire is an extreme response to ordinary eyes, and exactly right
    for the role. Finding it requires <strong>practical wisdom</strong> (<em>phronesis</em>):
    the judgement that reads a concrete situation, weighs what matters here, and sees the
    right act — the skill every other virtue depends on. Moral responsibility, on this view,
    attaches to what we do <em>voluntarily</em> and with knowledge, and grows with character:
    we are responsible for our virtues because, once adult, we are responsible for the habits
    that formed them. The classic objection is circularity ("right acts are what the virtuous
    would do" — and who decides?), and the standard reply is Aristotle's own anchor: the
    virtuous person is the one who reliably <em>gets eudaimonia-relevant judgement right</em>,
    whose verdicts survive reflection. Rosalind Hursthouse and others have made this
    framework fully modern.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that the virtues are innate — you are born brave or
  timid, honest or not, and character is a fixed temperament. Aristotle's entire practical
  programme denies this: virtues are <em>habits</em>, built by repeated action and destroyed
  the same way, which is precisely why he cares so much about upbringing, practice and who
  you imitate. The contrast matters morally: if character were innate, moral education would
  be pointless. On Aristotle's view it is the most important project there is — you are
  always, by acting, becoming someone.</p>

  <h2>One case, three verdicts</h2>
  <p>
    Take lying. A utilitarian asks: does this lie produce more wellbeing than honesty?
    (Usually no — trust is precious — but the Nazi-at-the-door case passes.) A Kantian
    refuses outright: a lie treats the deceived person as a tool, and universalised lying
    destroys language's point. A virtue ethicist asks what the truthful person would do — and
    finds a third answer: not lying, but also not volunteering everything; tact is a virtue
    too. Three frameworks, three different fault lines: consequences, consistency, character.
    And the wallet from the opening scene? Ask all three, and you have a much better
    examination of your own instinct than any single answer gives.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the difference between a consequentialist and a non-consequentialist answer to "what makes an act right?" in one sentence each.</li>
    <li>A city must choose between repairing a swimming pool used by 200 keen swimmers or a playground used occasionally by 2,000 families. Which of Bentham's dimensions does the debate actually turn on?</li>
    <li>Why does Kant think the honest-but-because-it-pays shopkeeper is not acting morally, even though he does the right thing?</li>
    <li>What is the doctrine of the mean — and why is it not "moderation in all things"?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Consequentialism: the act is right if it produces the best
    outcomes of the available options. Non-consequentialism: some acts are right or wrong in
    themselves — by rule or duty — regardless of outcome. (2) Extent (how many) versus
    intensity and frequency (how keenly, how often) — the same trade the park-versus-car-park
    case exposed; the numbers only matter once you know which dimensions you are counting.
    (3) Because moral worth lies in the motive: he acts from inclination (profit), not from
    duty. The action is correct but the will is not the good will Kant is defining. (4) A
    virtue is the midpoint between two vices relative to the situation and the person —
    courage between cowardice and recklessness. It is not averaging or restraint: sometimes
    the mean requires an extreme act, like a firefighter's courage.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Normative ethics asks what justifies a moral judgement — a question that survives "it is all opinion", because opinion-theories cannot explain reformers or even genuine disagreement.</li>
    <li>Three families: consequentialism looks at outcomes, deontology at duties and rules, virtue ethics at character.</li>
    <li>Bentham: pleasure counted impartially, via the felicific calculus; Mill: pleasures differ in quality; modern preference utilitarianism counts what people want.</li>
    <li>Utilitarianism's four standing problems: tyranny of the majority, the experience machine, calculation, and partiality.</li>
    <li>Kant: act only on maxims you could will as universal law; treat persons always as ends, never merely as means.</li>
    <li>Aristotle: eudaimonia is rational activity excellently performed over a whole life; virtues are habits found at the mean, guided by practical wisdom.</li>
    <li>The same act can get three different verdicts (lying) — which is why the professional skill is naming which framework, and which consideration, is doing the work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The three frameworks return everywhere in this subject: political philosophy builds on
    the utilitarian and Kantian traditions when it asks why anyone obeys the state; the
    ethics-of-technology lesson runs all three against algorithms and AI; and the epistemology
    lesson supplies the tool this page kept borrowing — how to justify any claim at all. The
    logic lesson, meanwhile, is the defence kit for everything here: each theory lives or dies
    by arguments, and arguments have shapes you can check.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The sharpest modern battleground is the <strong>trolley problem</strong> (Philippa Foot,
    1967, developed by Judith Jarvis Thomson): divert a runaway trolley to kill one instead of
    five? Most say divert — utilitarian arithmetic. Then the "footbridge" variant: push a
    large man off a bridge to stop the trolley, same arithmetic, and most refuse — the
    humanity formula speaks. The gap between the two verdicts is where double-effect
    doctrine, moral luck, and decades of experimental philosophy now live. Also deeper:
    Anscombe's 1958 essay "Modern Moral Philosophy" argued that "morally ought" had outlived
    its divine-law framework and that rule-worship and utility-mania both miss something —
    the paper that revived virtue ethics in the mainstream. And the professional takeaway is
    not to pick a team but to <em>name the framework</em> you are using when you judge: most
    public moral arguments are people in different frameworks shouting past each other, and
    the loudest progress happens when someone says which thing — outcome, duty or character —
    they think is decisive, and why.
  </p>

